THIS WEEK USES AWS (EKS). Equivalent AKS (Azure) and GKE (Google)
commands are listed in each day's lab. The concepts are the same.

COST WARNING: Cloud clusters cost money every hour they exist.
Our lab cluster (control plane + 2 small nodes + NAT gateway + load
balancers) is roughly USD 0.30-0.50 per hour. Delete everything at the
end of EVERY day. Set a budget alert TODAY before anything else.


1. SELF-MANAGED vs MANAGED
--------------------------
                     kubeadm (Week 6)        EKS / AKS / GKE
  Control plane      you run, patch, back up provider runs it (HA,
                                             etcd backups, upgrades)
  You pay for        your servers            control plane fee (EKS
                                             ~0.10 USD/h) + nodes
  Worker nodes       you                     node groups / node pools
                                             (VMs you choose), or
                                             serverless (Fargate,
                                             Autopilot, EKS Auto Mode)
  Integrations       you install             LB, disks, IAM, logs
                                             built in
  Access             SSH to control plane    no access to control
                                             plane machines at all
Almost all cloud production uses managed services.


2. CLOUD NETWORKING (VPC)
-------------------------
  VPC              your private network in a region (e.g. 10.0.0.0/16)
  Region / AZ      Mumbai = ap-south-1; AZs ap-south-1a/b/c are
                   separate data centres. Spread nodes over 2-3 AZs.
  Subnets          slices of the VPC in ONE AZ
    public subnet  has a route to an Internet Gateway (load balancers)
    private subnet no direct inbound internet; outbound via a NAT
                   gateway (worker nodes live here)
  Security groups  stateful firewalls on network interfaces

EKS networking: the AWS VPC CNI gives each POD a real VPC IP from the
node's subnet (Day 20). Pods are routable in the VPC without overlay,
but you can run out of subnet IPs; plan CIDRs big enough.


3. CLOUD IAM
------------
Two separate questions:
  a) Who can use the CLUSTER (kubectl)?
     EKS: IAM users/roles mapped to Kubernetes permissions through
          "access entries" (older: aws-auth ConfigMap).
     AKS: Microsoft Entra ID + Azure RBAC or Kubernetes RBAC.
     GKE: Google IAM + Kubernetes RBAC.
  b) What can PODS do in the cloud (read S3, write to a queue)?
     Workload identity: a Kubernetes ServiceAccount is linked to a
     cloud IAM role (EKS Pod Identity / IRSA, AKS Workload Identity,
     GKE Workload Identity). No static keys. (Day 47)


4. THE TOOLS
------------
  aws CLI      talk to AWS
  eksctl       create/delete EKS clusters with one command or a YAML
               file (uses CloudFormation behind the scenes)
  kubectl      same as always
  helm         package manager (used this week for add-ons; deep dive
               in Week 8)
Production teams usually use Terraform/OpenTofu instead of eksctl.


5. WHAT A MANAGED CLUSTER STILL LEAVES TO YOU
---------------------------------------------
Node OS updates (node group upgrades), add-ons (CNI, CSI drivers,
CoreDNS versions), autoscaling setup, ingress/LB controllers,
observability, backups of your apps' data, security policies, cost.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Provider runs the control plane; you run nodes and add-ons.
- Nodes in private subnets across AZs; LBs in public subnets.
- Cluster access IAM vs pod IAM are different things.
- Budget alert first, delete everything daily.
